WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.990
Hi everybody, Evan from PureButtons.com here
with a brief overview of rich black values

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.980
in the CMYK printing process.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.240
A lot of the time when setting up black in
Adobe Illustrator or or Photoshop, designers

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.910
will set it to what is called "100k Black".

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:24.790
This is equated as 0s in all of the colors
except for black 100%.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:28.910
You can also just grab the darkest color out
of the color picker and you get something

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:35.360
like this: 75/68/67/90.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.140
But what we like is something called "Rich
Black" and this is a value that we have created

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:47.010
based on tests with our printers to produce
the darkest, most natural-looking black value.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.410
This is 60% Cyan, 40% Magenta and Yellow,
and 100% Black.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.090
This produces a rich black color that is not
milky or too heavy.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:02.690
Finally, we have what's called "Registration
Black" which is seen as 100 all the way across

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.800
the board.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.420
NEVER USE REGISTRATION BLACK.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.750
What this does is it puts too much ink or
pigment on the page, it can create printing

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.890
abnormalities, with liquid based inks there
can be smearing due to increased dry time

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:20.360
that has increased, and it can also create
shiny patches in your design that are unwanted.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.040
Finally we must talk about how Illustrator
handles black values.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:30.210
I'm going to leave this text here at Registration
Black and then I'm going to draw a box behind

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:37.810
it and set that to 100K Black.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:46.340
Now, you would think that since the text is
at Registration Black, and the background

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:52.080
is at 100K Black, the background would be
lighter since there's less ink, but they look the same.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.110
So if I sent this file based on what it looks
like to the printer I would get back a document

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.580
that actually looks different.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.740
So we need to go to our Preferences and go
to the Appearance of Black menu, and by default

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.009
it says to Display All Blacks as Rich Black.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:11.150
You need to turn this to say Display All Blacks
Accurately.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.870
And this is what we would get back from the
printer.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:13.879
A HUGE surprise.

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:19.010
It's very important to work in the same type
of black across the entire project, and this

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.890
would be unacceptable.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:25.690
When using very small text it's okay to get
by with 100K Black.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:30.590
Because on a white or light background and
very small black text, you're not going to

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.610
notice if the text is not as dark as it could
be.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.580
You'll also have better resolution because
you don't have other layers of color that

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.560
are microscopically misaligned causing blurring.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.580
You simply have a single layer of 100% Black.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.200
This is the only instance I would recommend
using 100K Black.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:53.000
For everything else I would use Rich Black
which again is a combination of 60/40/40/100.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.940
And that's all- keep an eye on your black
values, and visit PureButtons.com for custom buttons and more.

